WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.686
If you want to understand any system, follow the money.

2
00:00:03.686 --> 00:00:09.785
California's school funding is famously complicated, but the core ideas fit in one episode.

3
00:00:09.785 --> 00:00:10.388
Let's go.

4
00:00:10.388 --> 00:00:11.453
First, how much?

5
00:00:11.453 --> 00:00:17.574
California's K–12 system spends well over a hundred billion dollars a year from all sources.

6
00:00:17.574 --> 00:00:26.157
Per student, that works out to roughly twenty thousand dollars or more in recent years, counting state, local, and federal money.

7
00:00:26.157 --> 00:00:30.149
That sounds like a lot, and compared to decades past, it is.

8
00:00:30.149 --> 00:00:37.534
But compared to other states, California has spent less per student than many for most of the last forty years.

9
00:00:37.534 --> 00:00:40.994
It has climbed toward the national average recently.

10
00:00:40.994 --> 00:00:44.653
And California's high costs mean each dollar buys less.

11
00:00:44.653 --> 00:00:48.712
Salaries, housing, construction, and land all cost more here.

12
00:00:48.712 --> 00:01:05.146
You can see that in the classroom, too: California's student-to-teacher ratio is one of the highest in the country, well above the national average, which means each dollar of spending is stretched across more kids per teacher than in most states.

13
00:01:05.146 --> 00:01:13.862
So when people argue about whether California spends enough, part of the disagreement is whether you adjust for what money can buy.

14
00:01:13.862 --> 00:01:14.993
What does it buy?

15
00:01:14.993 --> 00:01:15.925
Mostly people.

16
00:01:15.925 --> 00:01:28.500
Roughly eighty to eighty-five percent of a typical district's budget goes to salaries and benefits for teachers, aides, counselors, administrators, custodians, bus drivers, and other staff.

17
00:01:28.500 --> 00:01:34.222
That's why decisions about pay, pensions, and staffing ratios dominate school budgets.

18
00:01:34.222 --> 00:01:36.353
Where does the money come from?

19
00:01:36.353 --> 00:01:47.626
About two-thirds comes from the state, a smaller share from local property taxes, and roughly a tenth from the federal government, with some variation year to year.

20
00:01:47.626 --> 00:01:56.769
There's a fourth, much smaller source too: the California Lottery, a name everyone recognizes even though it barely moves the needle.

21
00:01:56.769 --> 00:02:08.180
It contributes around one percent of school budgets, a few hundred dollars per student, but it adds up — more than thirty-four billion dollars for schools since 1985.

22
00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:12.373
The state-local-federal split wasn't always the case, though.

23
00:02:12.373 --> 00:02:16.910
It changed because of two ballot measures you should know by name.

24
00:02:16.910 --> 00:02:19.935
The first is Proposition 13, passed in 1978.

25
00:02:19.935 --> 00:02:29.077
It capped property taxes at one percent of a property's value and limited how fast assessed values can rise until a property is sold.

26
00:02:29.077 --> 00:02:38.220
Before Prop 13, local property taxes were the main source of school money, and wealthy districts could spend far more than poor ones.

27
00:02:38.220 --> 00:02:45.300
That inequity had already reached the courts: in 1971, the California Supreme Court ruled in Serrano v.

28
00:02:45.300 --> 00:02:56.642
Priest that funding schools mainly through local property taxes was unconstitutional, because it let wealthy districts spend so much more per student than poor ones.

29
00:02:56.642 --> 00:03:02.554
Prop 13 didn't fix that inequity directly, but it did change who controlled the money.

30
00:03:02.554 --> 00:03:08.190
After Prop 13, local revenue dropped, and the state took over most school funding.

31
00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:13.071
Control over school money shifted from local communities to Sacramento.

32
00:03:13.071 --> 00:03:16.164
The second is Proposition 98, passed in 1988.

33
00:03:16.164 --> 00:03:26.476
It set a minimum funding guarantee for schools and community colleges, calculated through formulas tied to state revenue, enrollment, and the economy.

34
00:03:26.476 --> 00:03:34.656
In practice, it usually means a large share of the state's general fund, often around forty percent, goes to education.

35
00:03:34.656 --> 00:03:48.610
Prop 98 protects school funding, but it also means school budgets rise and fall with the state's tax revenue, which in California depends heavily on income taxes paid by high earners and can swing a lot.

36
00:03:48.610 --> 00:03:52.851
Once the state has its education money, how does it hand it out?

37
00:03:52.851 --> 00:03:57.490
Since 2013, mostly through the Local Control Funding Formula, or LCFF.

38
00:03:57.490 --> 00:04:01.466
It replaced dozens of separate programs with a simpler idea.

39
00:04:01.466 --> 00:04:06.105
Every district gets a base grant per student, adjusted by grade level.

40
00:04:06.105 --> 00:04:14.587
Then it gets extra money, called supplemental grants, for each student who is low-income, an English learner, or in foster care.

41
00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:22.539
Districts where those students make up more than fifty-five percent of enrollment get an additional concentration grant.

42
00:04:22.539 --> 00:04:32.082
The idea is that students with greater needs should bring more resources, and that local districts, not Sacramento, should decide how to use it.

43
00:04:32.082 --> 00:04:39.769
That's also why the LCAP from last episode exists: it's how districts show what they're doing with that extra money.

44
00:04:39.769 --> 00:04:42.022
Not everything flows through LCFF.

45
00:04:42.022 --> 00:04:47.589
Some money is still earmarked for specific purposes, often called categorical funds.

46
00:04:47.589 --> 00:04:52.095
The biggest is special education, which gets its own funding stream.

47
00:04:52.095 --> 00:04:58.457
Others support things like school meals, after-school programs, and the arts money from Prop 28.

48
00:04:58.457 --> 00:04:59.780
Is money well spent?

49
00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:03.153
It's a fair question, and the answer is: sometimes.

50
00:05:03.153 --> 00:05:16.382
Research now shows fairly clearly that money matters, especially for low-income students, when it's spent on things with good evidence behind them, like strong teachers, early education, and tutoring.

51
00:05:16.382 --> 00:05:20.681
It matters less when it goes to things that don't touch learning.

52
00:05:20.681 --> 00:05:26.502
Most districts aren't wasting large sums, but many could get more out of what they have.

53
00:05:26.502 --> 00:05:48.528
That's a separate question from outright misuse, though — districts undergo mandatory financial audits every year, and county offices of education provide additional oversight, so there's a formal check against money being flatly misused, apart from the harder question of whether every dollar is spent as effectively as it could be.

54
00:05:48.528 --> 00:05:53.489
If Californians want more for schools, there are only a few ways to get it.

55
00:05:53.489 --> 00:05:59.905
The state can raise taxes, which requires a two-thirds vote of the Legislature or voter approval.

56
00:05:59.905 --> 00:06:05.461
Voters have extended higher income tax rates on top earners through ballot measures.

57
00:06:05.461 --> 00:06:11.943
Locally, districts can ask voters to approve bonds for facilities and parcel taxes for operations.

58
00:06:11.943 --> 00:06:14.853
A parcel tax is a flat tax on each property.

59
00:06:14.853 --> 00:06:22.857
Most parcel taxes still need a two-thirds vote when a school board places the measure on the ballot, which is a high bar.

60
00:06:22.857 --> 00:06:38.335
But a 2021 ruling by the California Supreme Court opened another path: when a parcel tax reaches the ballot through voter initiative, meaning a citizen petition rather than a school board vote, it only needs a simple majority to pass.

61
00:06:38.335 --> 00:06:42.766
Communities that pass them can fund things the state doesn't cover.

62
00:06:42.766 --> 00:06:47.859
And there's one more resource that doesn't show up on the budget: volunteers.

63
00:06:47.859 --> 00:06:56.723
Parents, grandparents, and community members who tutor, coach, chaperone, and raise funds through PTAs and foundations add real value.

64
00:06:56.723 --> 00:07:02.874
It's a kind of stealth wealth, and it's unevenly distributed, which is worth keeping in mind.

65
00:07:02.874 --> 00:07:06.975
For more, see Chapter 8 of the free Ed100 course at ed100.org.
